🌐 Network Analysis Commands
These commands help inspect active network connections and listening ports.
lsof -i
Lists open network files and connections.
Bash
lsof -i
Example
Bash
lsof -i :80
Shows which process is using port 80.
netstat -tulnp
Displays listening ports and associated processes.
Bash
netstat -tulnp
Option Breakdown
Option
Meaning
t
TCP sockets
u
UDP sockets
l
Listening ports
n
Numeric output
p
Process info
ss -tulnp
Modern and faster replacement for netstat.
Bash
ss -tulnp
Advantages
Faster
Better filtering
Included in modern Linux systems
📁 File and Permission Analysis
Used to inspect file permissions, metadata, and privilege risks.
ls -lah
Detailed file listing.
Bash
ls -lah
Shows
Permissions
Owner
Group
Size
Hidden files
find / -perm -4000
Finds files with the SUID bit set.
Bash
find / -perm -4000 2>/dev/null
What is SUID?
SUID allows a file to run with the owner's privileges (often root).
Security Risk
Misconfigured SUID binaries can allow privilege escalation.
stat filename
Displays detailed metadata.
Bash
stat filename
Information Shown
Size
Permissions
Inode
Access time
Modification time
Change time
🔐 File Integrity Verification
Hashes are used to confirm that a file has not been altered.
md5sum
Generates an MD5 hash.
Bash
md5sum file.txt
MD5 is fast but not cryptographically secure.
sha256sum
Generates a SHA-256 hash.
Bash
sha256sum file.txt
Preferred For
Integrity verification
Secure file comparison
🔍 Example Hash Output
Plain text
9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015a3bf4f1b2b0b822cd15d6c15b0f00a08  file.txt
